\section{Study catalog}{

qesR ships a catalog of the studies it can load: \code{\link[=qes_studies]{qes_studies()}} lists
them, \code{\link[=qes_docs]{qes_docs()}} lists their documents and \code{\link[=qes_cite]{qes_cite()}} cites them, all
without a network request. Each study is pinned to one Dataverse dataset
version and one data file, identified by its md5 checksum.
}

\section{Data files}{

\code{\link[=get_qes]{get_qes()}} reads the original upload of each study's pinned data file
(SPSS or Stata), after checking its md5 and its rows and columns against
the catalog. Names, codes and missing values are those of the file;
labels come from the file (for \code{qes2012}, from the SPSS twin of the same
data, whose labels are complete). Codes an SPSS file declares as
user-missing are kept as values, with the declaration in the column
attributes \code{qes_na_values} and \code{qes_na_range}. The attribute
\code{qes_provenance} records which file was read; \code{\link[=qes_provenance]{qes_provenance()}} returns
it and prints it as a paragraph for a replication log.
\code{\link[=qes_download]{qes_download()}} saves the original files themselves (data file and
documents), md5-checked, in a directory you choose.
}

\section{Codebooks and search}{

The description of every variable (label, question text in English and
French, value labels, missing codes) ships with qesR for the studies
released under CC0, so \code{\link[=qes_codebook]{qes_codebook()}}, \code{\link[=qes_question]{qes_question()}} and
\code{\link[=qes_search]{qes_search()}} work offline. Question text comes from the deposited
questionnaires. \code{qes2022} (CC BY-NC 4.0) ships no metadata: its codebook
is built from your own copy of the data file and kept in the download
cache. \code{\link[=qes_missing]{qes_missing()}} sets "don't know", "refused" and declared missing
codes to \code{NA}.
}

\section{Network use}{

Requests go to the Dataverse servers listed in the catalog, one at a time
and at least one second apart per server. They carry the User-Agent
\verb{qesR/<version> R/<version>} and no other identifying information.
A request that fails for a passing reason (a timeout, a dropped
connection, HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503 or 504) is tried again, up to
\code{qesR.max_tries} attempts in all, after a growing pause, or after the delay
the server asks for in \code{Retry-After} (at most two minutes; a longer delay
is an error). A download that stalls is abandoned after
\code{qesR.stall_timeout} seconds. A file is saved under its final name only
once it is complete and, for catalog files, once its md5 matches the
catalog.

Some servers (Harvard Dataverse) may refuse automated requests. qesR does
not work around this: the error (class \code{qesR_error_http_refused}) says
where to save the file after downloading it in a web browser.

Downloaded catalog files are kept in a cache, by default only for the
session; see \code{\link[=qes_cache_info]{qes_cache_info()}} and \code{\link[=qes_cache_clear]{qes_cache_clear()}}.
}

